  "output": "Now I have a thorough picture of the codebase. Here's the complete performance analysis:\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis: HR Policy Bot\n\n### 1. N+1 / Redundant Database Connection Patterns\n\n**Issue: New SQLite connection opened per operation — `db.py`**\n\nEvery method in `DatabaseManager` calls `aiosqlite.connect(...)` in its own `async with` block. Under a single query request, this means: `save_query` → `get_agent_responses` (8 agents) → `save_orchestrator_response` → etc. each opens and closes a new connection. On spinning rust or a flash-constrained Jetson, connection setup overhead accumulates.\n\n```python\n# Current pattern (db.py:91-109) — new connection per call\nasync def save_query(self, record: QueryRecord) -> None:\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:  # open\n        await db.execute(...)\n        await db.commit()\n    # close\n\nasync def save_agent_response(self, record: AgentResponseRecord) -> None:\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:  # open again\n        ...\n```\n\n**Fix: Add a persistent connection with WAL mode**\n\n```python\nclass DatabaseManager:\n    def __init__(self, db_path: Path = _DB_PATH) -> None:\n        self._db_path = db_path\n        self._db_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        self._conn: aiosqlite.Connection | None = None\n\n    async def _get_conn(self) -> aiosqlite.Connection:\n        if self._conn is None:\n            self._conn = await aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path)\n            self._conn.row_factory = aiosqlite.Row\n            await self._conn.execute(\"PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL\")\n            await self._conn.execute(\"PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL\")\n        return self._conn\n\n    async def save_query(self, record: QueryRecord) -> None:\n        db = await self._get_conn()\n        await db.execute(\"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO queries ...\", (...))\n        await db.commit()\n\n    async def close(self) -> None:\n        if self._conn:\n            await self._conn.close()\n            self._conn = None\n```\n\nAlso add `PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL` — WAL mode allows concurrent reads alongside writes, which matters when agents are saving responses while the cache is being read.\n\n---\n\n**Issue: Missing database index on `agent_responses.query_id`**\n\n`get_agent_responses` does a full table scan filtered by `query_id` (`db.py:177`). With no index this is O(n) over all rows.\n\n```sql\n-- Add to _SCHEMA in db.py\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_agent_responses_query_id ON agent_responses(query_id);\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_orchestrator_responses_query_id ON orchestrator_responses(query_id);\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_fetch_cache_ttl ON fetch_cache(fetched_at);\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. No React — N/A\n\nThe frontend is vanilla JS, no React. Skipping re-render analysis.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Caching Opportunities\n\n**Issue: `_load_instruments` reads disk on every `SpecialistAgent` instantiation — `specialist.py:72-75`**\n\nThe `get_specialists()` singleton means this runs once at startup. However, the `json.load(open(...))` inside `_load_instruments` is called 8 times (once per agent), each reopening and parsing the same file.\n\n```python\n# Current — specialist.py:72-75 — file opened 8 times\ndef _load_instruments(self, agent_id: str) -> list[dict]:\n    with open(_REGISTRY_PATH) as f:\n        registry = json.load(f)\n    return [r for r in registry if agent_id in r[\"agent_ids\"]]\n```\n\n**Fix: Load registry once at module level**\n\n```python\n# At module level in specialist.py\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n_REGISTRY_PATH = Path(__file__).parent.parent / \"data\" / \"policy_registry.json\"\n_REGISTRY: list[dict] = json.loads(_REGISTRY_PATH.read_text())\n\nclass SpecialistAgent(GenericAgent):\n    def _load_instruments(self, agent_id: str) -> list[dict]:\n        return [r for r in _REGISTRY if agent_id in r[\"agent_ids\"]]\n```\n\n---\n\n**Issue: `_make_model` creates a new `GenerativeModel` object on every LLM call — `client.py:40-50`**\n\n`chat()`, `stream_chat()`, and `chat_parsed()` each call `self._make_model(...)`, which instantiates a new `genai.GenerativeModel`. For the orchestrator's two-phase flow (routing + synthesis), that's at minimum 2 + 8 = 10 model instantiations per query.\n\n```python\n# Current — client.py:70-76\nasync def chat(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, model: str | None = None) -> str:\n    m = self._make_model(model)  # new object every call\n    contents = [...]\n    return await self._generate(m, contents)\n```\n\n**Fix: Cache model instances by (model_name, json_output)**\n\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\nclass GeminiClient:\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=4)\n    def _get_model(self, model_name: str, json_output: bool) -> genai.GenerativeModel:\n        generation_config: dict = {\n            \"temperature\": self._config.model.temperature,\n            \"top_p\": self._config.model.top_p,\n        }\n        if json_output:\n            generation_config[\"response_mime_type\"] = \"application/json\"\n        return genai.GenerativeModel(model_name=model_name, generation_config=generation_config)\n\n    async def chat(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, model: str | None = None) -> str:\n        m = self._get_model(model or self._model_name, False)\n        ...\n```\n\n---\n\n**Issue: `content_hash` is recomputed on every `CacheEntry` property access — `cache.py:37-39`**\n\n```python\n@property\ndef content_hash(self) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(self.content.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()  # O(n) every time\n```\n\n`set_cached` accesses `entry.content_hash` once per cache write, so this is a single call per write — minor but worth noting. If content is large (up to 24,000 chars), hashing it on the hot path is wasted if the hash isn't used for deduplication. Since it's only written to DB as a metadata field and never checked for equality on read, it could be computed lazily at write time only (already effectively the case — this is low priority).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory Leak Risk\n\n**Issue: `_domain_semaphores` and `_domain_last_request` grow unbounded — `engine.py:43-47`**\n\n```python\n# engine.py:43-47\nself._domain_semaphores: dict[str, asyncio.Semaphore] = defaultdict(\n    lambda: asyncio.Semaphore(3)\n)\nself._domain_last_request: dict[str, float] = defaultdict(float)\n```\n\nEach unique domain encountered adds a permanent entry. For a policy bot hitting a fixed set of ~10 government domains this is fine in practice, but if any user-controlled URL routing ever reaches here, it would grow without bound. Worth bounding:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass BoundedDict(OrderedDict):\n    def __init__(self, maxsize: int, *args, **kwargs):\n        self._maxsize = maxsize\n        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\n    def __setitem__(self, key, value):\n        super().__setitem__(key, value)\n        if len(self) > self._maxsize:\n            self.popitem(last=False)\n\n# In FetchEngine.__init__:\nself._domain_last_request: dict[str, float] = BoundedDict(maxsize=100)\n```\n\n---\n\n**Issue: WebSocket `_connections` dict never cleans up stale entries on send failure — `websocket.py:43-53`**\n\n```python\nasync def send(self, query_id: str, message: dict[str, Any]) -> bool:\n    ...\n    except Exception as e:\n        await self.disconnect(query_id)  # removes on failure ✓\n        return False\n```\n\nThis is handled correctly — `disconnect` is called on exception. No leak here.\n\n---\n\n**Issue: `import` statements inside `process_query` — `websocket.py:155,185`**\n\n```python\nasync def process_query(...):\n    ...\n    from src.models.schemas import QueryRecord        # line 155 — inside async function\n    ...\n    from src.models.schemas import OrchestratorResponseRecord  # line 185\n```\n\nPython caches module imports, so these don't re-execute the module body. But the dict lookup overhead on every call is unnecessary. Move these to the top of the file.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Redundant Computations\n\n**Issue: `_build_synthesis_prompt` rebuilds citation strings that are also aggregated separately — `orchestrator.py:165-200` + `203-223`**\n\nThe orchestrator renders citations into the synthesis prompt (for LLM context), then re-aggregates them with `_aggregate_citations`. These are two separate O(n) passes over the same citation data. Since the aggregated citations override whatever the LLM returns (`orch_response.citations = self._aggregate_citations(specialist_responses)`), the LLM-generated citations in the JSON response are discarded. This means the synthesis prompt citation block (lines 182-194) is wasted context that inflates token usage without affecting the final output.\n\n**Fix: Remove the citation block from the synthesis prompt**\n\n```python\ndef _build_synthesis_prompt(self, query_text: str, specialist_responses: list[dict]) -> str:\n    parts = [f\"ORIGINAL USER QUERY: {query_text}\\n\", \"SPECIALIST RESPONSES:\\n\"]\n    for response in specialist_responses:\n        agent_id = response.get(\"agent_id\", \"unknown\")\n        findings = response.get(\"findings\", \"No findings reported\")\n        confidence = response.get(\"confidence\", \"unknown\")\n        parts.append(f\"\\n[{agent_id.upper()} Agent] (Confidence: {confidence})\\n\")\n        parts.append(f\"{findings}\\n\")\n        # Remove citation block — citations are aggregated separately, not from LLM output\n    parts.append(\"\\n\\nSynthesize these responses into a comprehensive answer. ...\")\n    return \"\".join(parts)\n```\n\nThis reduces prompt token count by the citation block size (potentially hundreds of tokens per query × 8 agents = ~1,000+ tokens saved per query at synthesis time).\n\n---\n\n**Issue: `wrapWithCitations` in `app.js:482-493` compiles a regex for each citation on every render**\n\n```javascript\nfunction wrapWithCitations(text, citations) {\n    let result = text;\n    citations.forEach((citation, index) => {\n        const search = citation.instrument_title.split(' ').slice(0, 3).join('');\n        result = result.replace(\n            new RegExp(`(${search.replace(...)})`, 'gi'),  // compiled fresh every time\n            `$1${marker}`\n        );\n    });\n    return result;\n}\n```\n\nThis function is called once per response, so it's not hot. But the regex also has a subtle bug: `split(' ').slice(0, 3).join('')` joins with no separator, making \"Financial Administration Act\" become `\"FinancialAdministrationAct\"` — a string that will never match the text. The citation markers will silently never appear. Fix:\n\n```javascript\nconst search = citation.instrument_title.split(' ').slice(0, 3).join(' ');\n//                                                              ↑ space, not empty string\n```\n\n---\n\n**Issue: `handleResponseChunk` does a full DOM query on every chunk — `app.js:173`**\n\n```javascript\nfunction handleResponseChunk(data) {\n    let messageEl = chatHistory.querySelector('.message.system:last-of-type');\n    // querySelector on every chunk\n```\n\nIf streaming produces many chunks, this is a repeated full DOM traversal. Cache the reference:\n\n```javascript\nlet activeMessageEl = null;  // module-level\n\nfunction handleResponseChunk(data) {\n    if (!activeMessageEl) {\n        activeMessageEl = createMessage('system', '', currentQueryId);\n        chatHistory.appendChild(activeMessageEl);\n    }\n    activeMessageEl.querySelector('.message-text').textContent += data.chunk;\n    scrollToBottom();\n}\n\n// Reset in resetProcessingState():\nfunction resetProcessingState() {\n    activeMessageEl = null;\n    ...\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Summary Priority Table\n\n| # | Location | Issue | Impact | Fix Effort |\n|---|----------|-------|--------|------------|\n| 1 | `db.py` | New SQLite connection per operation | Medium | Low |\n| 2 | `db.py` schema | Missing indexes on `query_id` foreign keys | High at scale | Trivial |\n| 3 | `specialist.py:72` | Policy registry file read 8× at startup | Low (startup) | Trivial |\n| 4 | `client.py:40` | `GenerativeModel` instantiated per LLM call | Low | Low |\n| 5 | `orchestrator.py:165` | Citation block in synthesis prompt wastes tokens | High (cost/latency) | Low |\n| 6 | `engine.py:43` | Unbounded `_domain_semaphores` dict | Low (bounded domain set) | Low |\n| 7 | `websocket.py:155,185` | In-function imports per request | Negligible | Trivial |\n| 8 | `app.js:482` | `wrapWithCitations` regex join bug (broken feature) | Bug, not perf | Trivial |\n| 9 | `app.js:173` | DOM query per streaming chunk | Low (streaming not used) | Low |\n\nThe highest-ROI changes are **#2** (add indexes — one-line schema change), **#5** (remove redundant citation block from synthesis prompt — saves ~1,000 tokens per query = real cost/latency reduction), and **#1** (persistent SQLite connection with WAL).\n",
